const QUESTIONS: readonly BankQuestion[] = [
  // Part I: The Lesson (chapters 1–3). Chapter 1: The Lesson
  q(1, 1, 'easy', 'What is Hazlitt’s one lesson of economics?', 'Trace every policy’s effects, for all groups', ['Never let governments spend money at all', 'Always buy low and sell high', 'Keep prices stable for everyone'],
    'The art of economics is looking not merely at the immediate but at the longer effects of any act or policy, and tracing its consequences not merely for one group but for all groups.', 'Look further, and look wider.'),
  q(1, 1, 'easy', 'Why is economics haunted by more fallacies than any other study, according to Hazlitt?', 'The special pleading of selfish interests', ['Economists are worse at math than others', 'Economic data are always out of date', 'Governments hide the true figures'],
    'Groups that gain from a policy argue for it plausibly and persistently, and hire “the best buyable minds” to make their case.', 'Who argues hardest for a policy?'),
  q(1, 1, 'medium', 'What separates the good economist from the bad one, for Hazlitt?', 'The good one looks beyond the immediate', ['The good one has better statistics', 'The good one trusts the market blindly', 'The good one advises governments'],
    'The bad economist sees only what immediately strikes the eye; the good economist also looks beyond, at the longer and indirect consequences, and at all groups.', 'What strikes the eye, and what doesn’t?'),
  q(1, 1, 'medium', 'How does Hazlitt answer “In the long run we are all dead”?', 'Today is yesterday’s long run', ['The long run never actually arrives', 'He agrees it’s a sound epigram', 'Only the short run can be known'],
    '“Today is already the tomorrow which the bad economist yesterday urged us to ignore.” We are already suffering the long-run consequences of past policies.', 'Whose long run are we living in now?'),
  q(1, 1, 'hard', 'Which opposite error does Hazlitt say the classical economists sometimes made?', 'Callousness toward groups hurt in the short run', ['Ignoring the long-run good of the community', 'Favoring tariffs over free trade', 'Trusting governments too much'],
    'They looked only at long-run results for the whole community. Few make that error today; the common one is the reverse.', 'Too far-sighted to notice who got hurt.'),
  q(1, 1, 'hard', 'Why do demagogues often sound more convincing than honest economists?', 'They tell half-truths that are simpler', ['They know more about economics', 'They use better and more recent statistics', 'The public distrusts all experts'],
    'They speak of the immediate effect, or the effect on one group, and as far as that goes they may be right. Showing all the effects on everyone takes a long, dull chain of reasoning.', 'What’s missing from their story?'),
  q(1, 1, 'medium', 'Nine-tenths of economic fallacies, Hazlitt says, stem from ignoring what?', 'Long-run and other-group effects', ['The rate of interest', 'The quantity of money in circulation', 'Comparative advantage'],
    'They look only at immediate consequences, or only at consequences for a particular group, or both.', 'The two halves of the one lesson.'),
  q(1, 1, 'hard', 'Hazlitt says the “new” economists sometimes find themselves in accord with whom?', 'Seventeenth-century mercantilists', ['The classical economists', 'The followers of Adam Smith and Ricardo', 'The physiocrats of France'],
    'Ignoring long-run effects, they fall back into ancient errors the classical economists had seemed to get rid of for good.', 'Old errors, back in fashion.'),

  // Chapter 2: The Broken Window
  q(1, 2, 'easy', 'A boy throws a brick through a baker’s window. The crowd says it makes business for the glazier. What does it forget?', 'The suit the baker now won’t buy', ['The cost of the brick itself', 'The glazier’s higher wages', 'The police who chase the boy'],
    'The baker had planned to spend that $50 on a suit. The glazier’s gain is merely the tailor’s loss, and the community is a suit poorer.', 'What else could the $50 have bought?'),
  q(1, 2, 'easy', 'Whose argument is Hazlitt borrowing for the broken window?', 'Bastiat’s', ['Keynes’s', 'Marx’s', 'Ricardo’s'],
    '“Let us begin with the simplest illustration possible: let us, emulating Bastiat, choose a broken pane of glass.”', 'A French economist of the 1800s.'),
  q(1, 2, 'medium', 'Why does the crowd never think of the tailor?', 'His suit will never be made, so no one sees it', ['The tailor is out of town that day', 'Tailors don’t buy from bakers', 'The suit would cost a great deal more than $50'],
    'They will see the new window in a day or two. They will never see the extra suit, precisely because it will never come into being.', 'What can’t be seen at all?'),
  q(1, 2, 'medium', 'Does the broken window add any employment, on balance?', 'No: the glazier’s gain is the tailor’s loss', ['Yes: the glazier gets $50 of work', 'Yes: the money circulates in widening circles', 'No: because glass is imported'],
    'The crowd imagines the $50 spreading in ever-widening circles. But the baker’s $50 would have spread just as widely from the tailor.', 'Would the $50 have been spent anyway?'),

  // Chapter 3: The Blessings of Destruction
  q(1, 3, 'easy', 'What does Hazlitt call the idea that war brings prosperity?', 'The broken-window fallacy, grown fat', ['A truth about wartime production', 'A theory proven by statistics', 'A new kind of economics'],
    '“It is merely our old friend, the broken-window fallacy, in new clothing, and grown fat beyond recognition.”', 'An old friend in new clothes.'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'War destruction creates enormous need. Why doesn’t that mean enormous demand?', 'Demand needs purchasing power too', ['Needs disappear once the war ends', 'Governments ration everything', 'Prices fall after every war'],
    'Need is not demand. China’s needs were incomparably greater than America’s, but its purchasing power, and the new business it could stimulate, far smaller.', 'Wanting something isn’t enough to buy it.'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'Most of the “good” results people credit to war really come from what, says Hazlitt?', 'Wartime inflation', ['New technology', 'Full employment', 'Government planning'],
    'Rising money totals make people feel richer though they may have less. The same effects could come from an equal peacetime inflation.', 'More money, but not more things.'),
  q(1, 3, 'hard', 'Rebuilding Europe’s houses will boost the construction business. What does Hazlitt add?', 'Demand is diverted from other things', ['Construction will boom for decades to come', 'The houses will all be imported', 'Builders will refuse the work'],
    'With more manpower and purchasing power going to houses, there is that much less left for everything else. Business up in one direction is down in another.', 'Where do the builders and the money come from?'),
  q(1, 3, 'hard', 'Why will total postwar demand usually shrink, according to Hazlitt?', 'Supply has shrunk, and supply is demand', ['People save all their money after a war', 'Governments raise taxes to repay war debts', 'Soldiers come home without any jobs'],
    'What people make is all they have to offer for what they want. The farmers’ wheat is their demand for cars. Destroy supply and you destroy demand.', 'What do people pay with, at bottom?'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'In what sense does supply create demand, for Hazlitt?', 'What people produce is what they trade', ['Advertising makes people want goods', 'Factories hire workers who then shop', 'Prices rise when supply grows'],
    'Demand and supply are two sides of the same coin. The supply of motor cars is the car workers’ demand for wheat and other goods.', 'Two sides of the same coin.'),
  q(1, 3, 'hard', 'What did John Stuart Mill and the classical writers see that many of Hazlitt’s contemporaries didn’t?', 'Through the veil of money to real goods', ['That wars always pay for themselves', 'That money is the only real wealth', 'That printing money creates jobs'],
    'Despite some lapses about money’s complex effects, they saw the underlying realities. Their critics were “befuddled by money rather than instructed by it.”', 'Money hides the real exchange of things.'),

  // Part II: Spending, Taxes and Credit (chapters 4–6). Chapter 4: Public Works Mean Taxes
  q(2, 4, 'easy', 'How must all government spending eventually be paid for, according to Hazlitt?', 'Out of taxes', ['Out of profits on state firms', 'Out of foreign aid', 'It never has to be paid for'],
    'Putting off the evil day only makes it worse, and inflation is itself a particularly vicious form of taxation.', 'Where does the government’s money come from?'),
  q(2, 4, 'easy', 'A $1,000,000 bridge is built “to provide employment.” What does Hazlitt say is not seen?', 'The private jobs its taxes destroyed', ['The bridge’s maintenance costs', 'The traffic it will cause', 'The workers’ wages being too low'],
    'For every public job created by the bridge, a private job has been destroyed somewhere else: fewer car workers, clothing workers, farmers.', 'Where did the $1,000,000 come from?'),
  q(2, 4, 'medium', 'When does Hazlitt have no objection to a public bridge?', 'When it’s needed more than what taxes cost', ['When it employs at least 500 people', 'Whenever unemployment is high', 'When a private firm refuses to build it'],
    'If it is even more necessary than the things taxpayers would have bought with the money, there is no objection. A bridge built mainly “to provide employment” is another matter.', 'Is it needed for its own sake?'),
  q(2, 4, 'medium', 'Once providing jobs becomes the goal of public works, what happens to the projects?', 'They must be invented, and need comes second', ['They are planned more carefully than ever', 'They are handed over to private firms', 'They pay for themselves in time'],
    'Instead of asking where bridges must be built, spenders ask where they can be built. And the more wasteful the work, the more jobs it “provides.”', 'Where must, or where can?'),
  q(2, 4, 'medium', 'What does Hazlitt see behind a region’s rise thanks to the Tennessee Valley Authority?', 'Other regions made poorer to pay for it', ['Proof private capital couldn’t do it', 'A net gain with no offsets anywhere', 'The success of local businessmen'],
    'Taxes taken from the whole country and spent in one section make that section comparatively richer. The thing “private capital could not have built” was built with private capital taken in taxes.', 'Where did the money come from?'),
  q(2, 4, 'hard', 'How does Hazlitt use Shaw’s Saint Joan, where a character calls Pythagoras a fool: “Couldn’t he use his eyes?”', 'To mock those who trust only what they see', ['To praise practical, hands-on men', 'To show that theory is often wrong', 'To compare economists with ancient astronomers'],
    'Champions of public housing point to the houses and dismiss the unbuilt homes and unmade goods as mere theory.', 'Some truths can’t be seen with the eyes.'),
  q(2, 4, 'hard', 'Public housing can be funded by yearly rent subsidies instead of a lump sum. Does that change Hazlitt’s point?', 'No: the cost is just spread over years', ['Yes: then no one pays for it', 'Yes: subsidies create real wealth', 'No: because rents are always too high'],
    'What is taken from taxpayers is merely spread over many years instead of concentrated in one. Such technicalities are irrelevant to the main point.', 'Spreading a cost out doesn’t erase it.'),
  q(2, 4, 'medium', 'Why do public works win the argument with most people, according to Hazlitt?', 'The works are visible; the losses are not', ['They really do create net wealth for the nation', 'They are always built very well', 'Taxpayers rarely object to them'],
    'We can see the bridge we cross every day. To see the homes, cars and coats never made takes an imagination few people keep up.', 'Can you photograph what was never made?'),

  // Chapter 5: Taxes Discourage Production
  q(2, 5, 'easy', 'Government spenders talk as if taxes move money from the nation’s right pocket to its left. What do they forget?', 'The effects on those who are taxed', ['That most taxes are voluntary', 'That money loses value over time', 'That corporations pay no taxes'],
    'They take money from A to pay B. “B is seen; A is forgotten.”', 'Whose money is B spending?'),
  q(2, 5, 'medium', 'A firm keeps 60 cents of each dollar it gains but loses the whole dollar when it loses. What follows?', 'It takes fewer risks and expands less', ['It takes more risks to make up the tax', 'It raises its workers’ wages', 'Nothing changes in its policies'],
    'It expands only where risk is minimal, and others decide not to start enterprises at all. Better machinery arrives more slowly, and real wages are held down.', 'Heads they win less, tails you lose it all.'),
  q(2, 5, 'medium', 'How do very high income taxes create the unemployment spenders say they’ll solve?', 'They stop the capital for new jobs forming', ['They make workers too lazy to apply for jobs', 'They push all employers abroad', 'They cause prices to fall too far'],
    'Capital is taxed away before it accumulates, and what remains is discouraged from new ventures. People ask why work most of the year for the government.', 'Where do new jobs come from?'),
  q(2, 5, 'hard', 'Does Hazlitt oppose all taxation?', 'No: reasonable taxes for essential services', ['Yes: every tax destroys production', 'No: he wants only the rich to pay any taxes', 'Yes: but only income taxes'],
    'Services that safeguard production more than compensate for modest taxes. The larger the share taken, the greater the deterrent, until no harmless tax can be devised.', 'How large a share is being taken?'),

  // Chapter 6: Credit Diverts Production
  q(2, 6, 'easy', 'Proposals for more “credit,” Hazlitt says, are just another name for what?', 'More debt', ['More money', 'More saving', 'More wealth'],
    'All credit is debt. Such proposals would seem far less inviting if they were called by that second name.', 'What must every honest borrower do eventually?'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'When the government lends to farmers, what is really being lent, according to Hazlitt?', 'Capital: the farm or tractor itself', ['Money, freshly printed for them', 'Time to pay off old debts', 'Nothing: the loans are free'],
    'Farms and tractors are limited. The farm lent to B cannot be lent to A, so the question is who gets it.', 'Money is only the medium of exchange.'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'Why are government loans likely to go to poorer risks than private ones?', 'Lending to those banks refuse is the point', ['Civil servants can’t read balance sheets', 'Private banks lend only to friends', 'Government loans carry higher interest'],
    'The whole argument for government lending is that it will lend to people who couldn’t get private loans: risks no one will take with his own money.', 'Why else would the government step in?'),
  q(2, 6, 'hard', 'Hazlitt says monetary cranks think credit is something a banker gives a man. What is it really?', 'Something the man already has', ['A gift of the central bank', 'A right every citizen holds', 'Money the banker creates'],
    'He has it through marketable assets or through his character and record. He brings it into the bank with him; the banker only swaps a more liquid asset for a less liquid one.', 'He brings it into the bank with him.'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'Who loses when a government loan sets up B as a farmer?', 'A, who would have got the farm', ['The taxpayers only, in the short run', 'Nobody: a new farm was created', 'The bank that refused B a loan'],
    'A, the proven farmer, is squeezed out by higher interest, higher farm prices or simply no farm left. Capital goes to the less efficient.', 'There is only so much farmland.'),
  q(2, 6, 'hard', 'Why are private lenders likely to make fewer mistakes than government lenders?', 'Bad lenders lose their money and stop lending', ['They have access to secret, inside information', 'The law forbids them from failing', 'They lend only to big companies'],
    'Private lenders face “a cruel market test.” Government lenders are those who pass civil service exams or explain best why it wasn’t their fault.', 'What happens to a lender who keeps losing?'),
  q(2, 6, 'hard', 'If the government bears businesses’ risks, what demand does Hazlitt say will follow?', 'For socialism: why not take the profits too?', ['For lower taxes on all businesses', 'For an immediate return to the gold standard', 'For more competition among banks'],
    'Taxpayers taking the risks while capitalists keep the profits can’t be justified. It also breeds favoritism, bribes and scandals.', 'Risk for the public, profit for whom?'),
  q(2, 6, 'easy', 'When the government gives loans or subsidies to business, what is it really doing?', 'Taxing successful firms to prop up failing ones', ['Creating new wealth out of nothing at all, by fiat', 'Helping every business equally', 'Spending money it saved earlier'],
    'It can give business nothing it doesn’t first or finally take from business, through taxes.', 'Where do its funds come from?'),

  // Part III: Machines and Jobs (chapters 7–10). Chapter 7: The Curse of Machinery
  q(3, 7, 'easy', 'What does Hazlitt call one of the hardiest of all economic delusions?', 'That machines create unemployment', ['That free trade creates monopolies', 'That saving causes depressions', 'That taxes encourage production'],
    'Destroyed a thousand times, it has risen a thousand times from its ashes. Held consistently, it would make every labor-saving step since primitive man a calamity.', 'Blamed whenever unemployment runs high.'),
  q(3, 7, 'medium', 'Adam Smith said a workman without machinery could hardly make one pin a day, but with it, how many?', '4,800', ['48', '480', '48,000'],
    'If machines merely threw men out of work, Hazlitt jokes, the pin industry already had 99.98 percent unemployment in Smith’s time.', 'Thousands, from a single worker.'),
  q(3, 7, 'hard', 'In 1760, when Arkwright invented his spinning machinery, England had 7,900 cotton spinners and weavers. How many by 1787?', '320,000', ['9,000', '32,000', '1,600,000'],
    'An increase of 4,400 percent in twenty-seven years, though the invention had been opposed as a threat to workers’ livelihoods.', 'A rise of 4,400 percent.'),
  q(3, 7, 'medium', 'Were the stocking knitters who smashed the new frames irrational, according to Hazlitt?', 'Not for themselves, but wrong about the long run', ['Yes: the new machines made all of them better off', 'No: the industry shrank for good', 'Yes: they were paid by the inventors'],
    'Most of the 50,000 knitters and their families suffered for forty years. Yet by the century’s end the industry employed at least 100 men for every one it had at the start.', 'Short run versus long run.'),
  q(3, 7, 'medium', 'A machine lets a coat maker cut labor in half. Where do the jobs reappear, first of all?', 'Making the machine, and wherever profits go', ['Nowhere at all: those jobs are simply gone for good', 'In government relief programs', 'Only in the coat industry itself'],
    'His extra profits go to more machines, other investments or his own spending. Unless he hoards, he gives indirectly as many jobs as he ceased to give directly.', 'What does he do with his savings?'),
  q(3, 7, 'hard', 'Coats fall from $50 to $30, and not a single extra coat is sold. Does employment still recover?', 'Yes: buyers spend the $20 elsewhere', ['No: coat demand must be elastic', 'No: the $20 is simply lost', 'Only if coat prices rise again'],
    'The new employment doesn’t depend on elastic demand for the product. Each buyer has $20 left to spend on something else.', 'What happens to the money people save?'),
  q(3, 7, 'medium', 'What is the real result of machines, for Hazlitt?', 'More production and higher living standards', ['More jobs than there would otherwise be', 'Higher profits only for capitalists', 'Shorter lives for factory workers'],
    'Creating jobs is no trick: full, back-breaking employment marks the most backward nations. Machines raise real wages, by lifting pay, cutting prices, or both.', 'Not jobs as such.'),
  q(3, 7, 'hard', 'Some writers say “Keep your eye on Joe Smith,” the worker a machine displaced. What does Hazlitt reply?', 'Keep one eye on him, and see the others too', ['Forget him: the market will provide for him', 'Ban the machine to protect him', 'Joe Smith never really exists'],
    'They forget Tom Jones who makes the machine, Ted Brown who runs it, and Daisy Miller who buys a coat for half the price. But Joe Smith’s lost skill is a real tragedy.', 'How many people does the machine affect?'),
  q(3, 7, 'hard', 'Which public figure wrote in 1945 that labor-saving devices are good only if they don’t cost workers their jobs?', 'Eleanor Roosevelt', ['John Maynard Keynes', 'Harry Truman', 'Henry Ford'],
    'Hazlitt quotes her syndicated column of September 19, 1945. Taken seriously, it would condemn all past technical progress too.', 'She wrote a syndicated newspaper column.'),

  // Chapter 8: Spread-the-Work Schemes
  q(3, 8, 'easy', 'What false belief lies behind spread-the-work schemes?', 'There’s a fixed amount of work to do', ['Machines always create more jobs', 'Workers prefer working longer hours', 'Unions make firms more efficient'],
    'There is no limit to the work to be done as long as any human need or wish that work could fill remains unsatisfied.', 'Is the amount of work fixed?'),
  q(3, 8, 'medium', 'A householder must hire two men for a job one could do. What does the country lose?', 'The sweater he can no longer afford', ['Nothing: two men now have work', 'Only some of the plumber’s time', 'The tax revenue from the extra worker'],
    'The extra tilesetter’s day means a day less for a sweater knitter. The householder has the repaired shower and no sweater; the country is a sweater short.', 'What doesn’t he buy now?'),
  q(3, 8, 'medium', 'The working week is cut from 40 to 30 hours with no change in hourly pay. Even at best, what happens?', 'Employed workers subsidize the newly hired', ['Total payrolls rise by a third', 'Production jumps across the board, in every trade', 'Everyone earns the same as before'],
    'There are no more man-hours, no more output, no larger payroll. The old workers each take home three-fourths as much to pay for others’ jobs.', 'Is the total amount of work any larger?'),
  q(3, 8, 'hard', 'What if the week is cut to 30 hours and hourly pay raised 33⅓ percent so weekly pay stays the same?', 'Costs rise and unemployment grows', ['Everyone keeps his job and his pay', 'Production rises to cover the cost', 'Prices fall as demand grows'],
    'If workers could be paid that much more, they could have had the raise on a 40-hour week. Otherwise the least efficient firms close and the least efficient workers lose their jobs.', 'Can firms afford a third more per hour?'),
  q(3, 8, 'medium', 'What did the Federal Wage-Hour Law’s 50 percent overtime premium aim at, besides higher pay?', 'Forcing employers to hire more workers', ['Protecting workers’ health', 'Raising more payroll taxes for the state', 'Cutting the price of goods'],
    'It wasn’t mainly about health or efficiency: it hoped to discourage employing anyone beyond forty hours, so that more people would be hired.', 'Spread the work around.'),

  // Chapter 9: Disbanding Troops and Bureaucrats
  q(3, 9, 'easy', 'After a war, people fear demobilized soldiers will find no jobs. What do they forget?', 'Taxpayers keep the money that paid the troops', ['Soldiers don’t want civilian jobs', 'The factories are all destroyed in every great war', 'The army will keep paying them'],
    'With the budget balanced, the government stops supporting the soldiers and the taxpayers keep those funds to buy more goods, giving the soldiers work.', 'Who paid for the army, and what will they do now?'),
  q(3, 9, 'medium', 'Why is the country actually richer when unneeded soldiers become civilians?', 'They produce instead of being supported', ['They spend more than soldiers do', 'Civilians pay lower taxes than soldiers', 'Veterans get government pensions'],
    'Soldiers supported by civilians become self-supporting civilians, trading goods and services for what they get.', 'From supported to self-supporting.'),
  q(3, 9, 'medium', 'Cutting unneeded government officials is called “deflationary.” What’s Hazlitt’s answer?', 'Taxpayers gain what the officials lose', ['Officials’ spending is vital to recovery', 'Washington will suffer most', 'It really is deflationary, but worth it'],
    'If Washington’s shops lose trade, shops elsewhere gain at least as much. And the officials, in private jobs, become productive.', 'Whose purchasing power goes up?'),
  q(3, 9, 'hard', 'Hazlitt says the “purchasing power” argument for keeping officials could equally defend whom?', 'A thief who robs you', ['A charity that helps you', 'A bank that lends to you', 'A doctor who treats you'],
    'The thief spends your money in bars and shops, but you spend that much less. When taxes support needless bureaucrats, you likewise get nothing back.', 'He spends your money too.'),
  q(3, 9, 'medium', 'Which public employees does Hazlitt say are as productive as anyone in private industry?', 'Needed police, firemen and judges', ['All civil servants, without exception', 'None: all government work is waste', 'Only those elected by voters'],
    'Their justification lies in the usefulness of their services, which let industry work in law, order and peace, not in their “purchasing power.”', 'Their services, not their spending.'),

  // Chapter 10: The Fetish of Full Employment
  q(3, 10, 'easy', 'What is the real economic goal of a nation, for Hazlitt?', 'Maximum production', ['Full employment', 'Stable prices', 'Equal incomes for everyone'],
    'Production is the end, employment merely the means. Full employment is a by-product of maximum production, not the other way round.', 'The end, not the means.'),
  q(3, 10, 'medium', 'Why is full employment, taken alone, a poor goal?', 'Coercion can provide it without wealth', ['It always causes inflation', 'It is impossible to achieve in a free market', 'Workers don’t want it'],
    'Hitler’s armament program, slave labor, prisons and chain gangs all had full employment. Primitive tribes have no unemployment and are wretchedly poor.', 'Prisons have full employment.'),
  q(3, 10, 'medium', 'The progress of civilization, Hazlitt says, has meant what for employment?', 'Its reduction', ['Its increase', 'No change', 'Its abolition'],
    'Growing wealth let America all but end child labor, free many of the old from work, and spare millions of women from needing jobs.', 'Who no longer has to work?'),
  q(3, 10, 'hard', 'What did Hazlitt say was considered a mark of genius in running the WPA?', 'Projects using the most men per dollar of work', ['Projects that finished under budget', 'Projects that private firms could never undertake', 'Projects that paid for themselves'],
    'Labor was praised for being least efficient. Better, if that were the choice, undisguised relief than make-work that disorganizes production.', 'The less efficient, the better.'),
  q(3, 10, 'hard', 'What is the real question about jobs in America in 1950, according to Hazlitt?', 'How much we will produce', ['Whether there will be 60 million jobs', 'How many will work for the state', 'Whether wages will keep rising'],
    'The problem of distribution, on which all the stress was being put, is more easily solved the more there is to distribute.', 'Jobs, or output?'),

  // Part IV: Trade and Protection (chapters 11–14). Chapter 11: Who’s “Protected” by Tariffs?
  q(4, 11, 'easy', 'On what one proposition did Adam Smith rest his case for free trade, as Hazlitt quotes it?', 'People should buy from whoever sells cheapest', ['Nations must export more than they import', 'Every country should be self-sufficient', 'Governments should pick winning industries'],
    '“In every country it always is and must be the interest of the great body of the people to buy whatever they want of those who sell it cheapest.”', 'Where would you rather shop?'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', '“What is prudence in the conduct of every private family can scarce be folly in that of a great kingdom.” Prudence about what?', 'Not making at home what’s cheaper to buy', ['Saving a part of every year’s income', 'Never borrowing from neighbors', 'Growing your own food in hard times'],
    'The tailor doesn’t make his own shoes; the shoemaker doesn’t make his own clothes. Each does what he does best and buys the rest.', 'Does the tailor make his own shoes?'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'The tariff on British sweaters is removed and a $15 American sweater can now be had for $10. What isn’t seen?', 'The $5 each buyer spends elsewhere', ['The British workers’ low wages', 'The sweaters’ poorer quality', 'The lost tariff revenue'],
    'Consumers get the same sweater and $5 left over to spend on other American goods, providing jobs in many other industries.', 'What happens to the money buyers save?'),
  q(4, 11, 'hard', 'Why must letting in more British goods eventually mean more American exports?', 'The British can use their dollars only here', ['Treaties require trade to balance', 'The British government buys them all with gold', 'Tariffs fall on both sides at once'],
    'The dollars the British earn selling to us are of use only to buy American goods. American employment doesn’t fall on net; production in both countries rises.', 'What can the British buy with dollars?'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'A $5 tariff brings a new sweater industry with 20,000 jobs. What does Hazlitt say happens elsewhere?', '20,000 fewer jobs in other industries', ['20,000 more jobs, from rising demand', 'Nothing at all changes elsewhere', 'Wages rise in all other industries'],
    'Consumers pay $5 more per sweater and have that much less for everything else. The new industry is visible; the shrinkage of a hundred others is not.', 'Where does the extra $5 per sweater come from?'),
  q(4, 11, 'hard', 'What does a tariff do to real wages, on net, according to Hazlitt?', 'It lowers them', ['It raises them', 'It leaves them alone', 'It doubles them'],
    'It may lift wages in the protected industry, but by shifting labor and capital to what they do less efficiently, it lowers average productivity and real wages.', 'What happens when resources are used less efficiently?'),
  q(4, 11, 'hard', 'How does Hazlitt compare tariffs to cheaper shipping?', 'We cut freight costs $1, then add $2 of tariff', ['Tariffs pay for better ships and roads', 'Tariffs make shipping far safer', 'Shipping always costs far more than any tariff does'],
    'Tariff walls act like real walls, or like tank traps against an invading army. They waste the investment in efficient transport.', 'Tank traps against “invading” goods.'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'A prohibitive tariff cuts off all imports. Which American industries suffer most, at first?', 'Exporters, like cotton and farm machinery', ['The protected sweater makers', 'Only foreign-owned firms', 'None: every American industry benefits from it'],
    'Foreigners earn no dollars, so they can buy nothing from us. Industries suffer in proportion to their sales abroad.', 'Who sold the most abroad?'),
  q(4, 11, 'hard', 'Does Hazlitt deny that a tariff can help anyone?', 'No: it can help the protected group', ['Yes: it helps no one at all', 'Yes: even the protected firms lose out', 'No: it helps every producer'],
    'If one industry alone were protected, it could gain on net. As protection spreads, everyone suffers from others’ protection. Removing a tariff helps the nation, but someone is hurt.', 'Somebody does gain, at others’ expense.'),

  // Chapter 12: The Drive for Exports
  q(4, 12, 'easy', 'What must imports and exports do in the long run, according to Hazlitt?', 'Equal each other', ['Exports must exceed imports', 'Imports must exceed exports', 'They have no relationship'],
    'It is exports that pay for imports, and vice versa. Without imports, foreigners have no funds to buy our goods.', 'How do foreigners pay for our goods?'),
  q(4, 12, 'medium', 'An American exporter is paid in British pounds. Why can’t that be the end of it?', 'He needs dollars, so imports must follow', ['Pounds are illegal to hold here', 'The bank charges far too much to swap them', 'The British pay only in gold'],
    'He can’t pay wages with pounds. He must buy British goods or sell the pounds to an American importer. Foreign exchange is a clearing of debts against credits.', 'What can he do with pounds?'),
  q(4, 12, 'medium', 'A car company lends a man $1,000 for a car that cost $900 to make, and gets back only $500. What’s the result?', 'A net loss of $400', ['A profit of $100', 'A gain from the sale', 'Breaking exactly even'],
    'It hasn’t made up in trade what it lost in bad loans. A nation is no different: it can’t grow rich by giving goods away.', 'Cost $900, got back $500.'),
  q(4, 12, 'hard', 'Who might gain from bad foreign loans that boost exports, and who pays?', 'Some exporters gain; taxpayers lose', ['Everyone gains through more trade', 'Foreign buyers lose the most in the end', 'Only bankers lose anything'],
    'For every extra dollar foreigners spend on our goods, domestic buyers end up with a dollar less in taxes. Even many exporters lose on net.', 'Who pays for loans that aren’t repaid?'),
  q(4, 12, 'medium', 'What is an export subsidy, in Hazlitt’s terms?', 'Giving foreigners something for nothing', ['A tax on goods sent abroad', 'A loan to a friendly foreign government', 'A reward for efficient firms'],
    'Selling goods for less than they cost to make is another case of trying to get rich by giving things away.', 'Who gets the discount?'),
  q(4, 12, 'hard', 'Does Hazlitt oppose giving aid to starving foreign peoples?', 'No, but call it charity, not business', ['Yes: all foreign aid is waste', 'Yes: it hurts American exports in the end', 'No: it always pays for itself'],
    'We should give generously for humane reasons. But bestowing charity under the impression it’s a hardheaded business deal leads to misunderstandings later.', 'Know what you are doing.'),

  // Chapter 13: “Parity” Prices
  q(4, 13, 'easy', 'What period did the farm lobby choose as the basis for “parity” prices?', '1909 to 1914', ['1929 to 1933', '1919 to 1924', '1939 to 1945'],
    'It was among the most favorable periods to agriculture in American history: not a random choice.', 'Just before the First World War.'),
  q(4, 13, 'medium', 'Why does Hazlitt say parity, applied to cars, would be absurd?', 'A 1942 Chevrolet would cost $3,270, not $907', ['Cars would be too cheap to make', 'Hardly anyone at all bought cars in 1909–1914', 'Farmers don’t buy cars'],
    'The car was incomparably better and cheaper to make. But farm productivity had soared too: cotton went from 188 to 260 pounds an acre.', 'Productivity rose in farming too.'),
  q(4, 13, 'medium', 'Wheat is pushed from $1 to $1.50 a bushel to help farmers buy more from industry. What does Hazlitt point out?', 'City workers pay 50 cents more for bread', ['Industry gains twice over', 'Farmers spend it all on wheat', 'Bread prices fall again within a year or so'],
    'Whatever extra purchasing power the farmer gains, the city worker loses. Industry loses in city sales what it gains in rural sales.', 'Who pays the extra 50 cents?'),
  q(4, 13, 'hard', 'Why is the parity policy a net loss, not just a transfer, according to Hazlitt?', 'It forces less food to be produced', ['It raises the farmers’ taxes', 'It lowers the price of bread for city workers', 'It sends money abroad'],
    'Prices are raised by cutting output, as in burning Brazil’s coffee or restricting acreage under the AAA. Less food means wealth destroyed.', 'How do you raise a price artificially?'),
  q(4, 13, 'medium', 'What shows parity was a special-interest device, not a principle, for Hazlitt?', 'It only ever worked one way', ['It was passed by a close vote', 'Economists designed it', 'It applied to all goods'],
    'When farm prices went above parity, the farm bloc never asked to bring them down or repay the subsidy. Nor was the principle extended to other goods.', 'What happened when prices went above parity?'),
  q(4, 13, 'hard', 'Defenders called parity the farmer’s compensation for industrial tariffs. Hazlitt’s verdict on the two together?', 'Both gain at the Forgotten Man’s cost', ['They balance each other out fairly in the end', 'Both help the economy as a whole', 'Parity is fine, but tariffs are not'],
    'A city worker paying more for blankets isn’t “compensated” by paying more for food; he’s robbed twice. End both systems instead.', 'Who pays for both?'),

  // Chapter 14: Saving the X Industry
  q(4, 14, 'easy', 'What does Hazlitt say is just as necessary for a dynamic economy as letting new industries grow?', 'Letting dying industries die', ['Subsidizing every industry equally', 'Protecting old skills by law', 'Freezing prices in old industries'],
    'Old industries must shrink to release capital and labor for new ones. Keeping the horse-and-buggy trade alive would have slowed the automobile.', 'Where do new industries get their workers?'),
  q(4, 14, 'medium', 'Under the Guffey Act, how many separate coal prices did the government end up fixing?', '350,000', ['35', '3,500', 'Just one'],
    'Different sizes, thousands of mines and destinations, and rail, truck, ship and barge shipments. Keeping coal dear also sped the switch to oil, gas and hydroelectric power.', 'It started out to fix “the” price of coal.'),
  q(4, 14, 'hard', 'What did “saving silver” do to China, according to Hazlitt?', 'Deflation, forcing it off silver', ['It made China rich almost overnight', 'It had no effect on China at all', 'It led China to adopt gold'],
    'One argument was that it would help “the East.” The Treasury also had to hoard unneeded silver bought far above market prices.', 'China was on a silver basis.'),
  q(4, 14, 'medium', 'If an industry really is overcrowded, does it need laws to keep newcomers out?', 'No: newcomers avoid dying industries', ['Yes: or the whole industry will collapse', 'Yes: newcomers always rush in', 'Only in wartime'],
    'Forcing capital and labor out pushes them into even worse uses, lowering production and living standards.', 'Who rushes into a dying business?'),
  q(4, 14, 'hard', 'What is the one advantage Hazlitt sees in a direct subsidy over tariffs or price-fixing?', 'It makes the cost to taxpayers obvious', ['It costs far less than any tariff would', 'It creates more jobs overall', 'It helps every industry at once'],
    'There is less room for the intellectual fog that surrounds tariffs and minimum prices. Plainly, taxpayers lose what the industry gains.', 'At least you can see who pays.'),

  // Part V: Prices and Price-Fixing (chapters 15–17). Chapter 15: How the Price System Works
  q(5, 15, 'easy', 'Which fallacy does Hazlitt say underlies the “production for use, not for profit” school?', 'The fallacy of isolation', ['The fallacy of composition', 'The gambler’s fallacy', 'The labor theory of value'],
    'It looks at one industry as if it existed alone. Every industry exists in relation to all the others.', 'Looking at one tree, not the forest.'),
  q(5, 15, 'easy', 'How is the problem of what to produce, and how much, solved in a modern economy?', 'Through the price system', ['By engineers and technicians', 'By a central planning board', 'By the labor unions'],
    'Prices, costs and profits keep shifting labor and capital among thousands of needs of different urgency, far better than bureaucrats could.', 'What tells producers what people want most?'),
  q(5, 15, 'medium', 'Why does Hazlitt bring in Robinson Crusoe and the Swiss Family Robinson?', 'To show one job expands only at others’ cost', ['To show that trade between people is unnecessary', 'To praise self-sufficiency', 'To show families need no prices'],
    'The wood-gatherer can’t complain he’d gather more if his brother helped all day instead of fishing for dinner. Every use of labor forgoes another.', 'More firewood means fewer fish.'),
  q(5, 15, 'hard', 'Are prices determined by costs of production, according to Hazlitt?', 'No: by supply and demand', ['Yes: always and exactly', 'Yes: by labor costs alone', 'No: by government decree'],
    'Past costs can’t set value. Expected future costs affect how much is made, so price and marginal cost tend toward each other, but cost doesn’t directly fix price.', 'Does what it cost yesterday matter to buyers today?'),
  q(5, 15, 'medium', 'What does Hazlitt compare the private enterprise system to?', 'Interlinked engines, each with a governor', ['A single giant machine run from the center', 'An army under one general', 'A lottery with many players'],
    'Like the centrifugal governor on a steam engine, each departure from balance sets off forces that correct it, through prices and profits.', 'A device that slows an engine running too fast.'),
  q(5, 15, 'hard', 'Critics ask why manufacturers stop making shoes once more aren’t profitable. Hazlitt’s answer?', 'Other needs are more urgent than more shoes', ['Shoemakers are simply too greedy to make more', 'Machines can’t make more shoes', 'People don’t actually need shoes'],
    'It would be idiotic to pile up mountains of shoes while coats, homes and bread went short. Shrinking one line releases resources for others.', 'What else needs making?'),
  q(5, 15, 'medium', 'How does Hazlitt define the costs of production?', 'The things given up to make something', ['The money a firm pays its workers', 'The price set by the government', 'The value of the finished goods'],
    'Leisure, pleasures, raw materials with other possible uses: everything is produced at the expense of forgoing something else.', 'What do you give up?'),

  // Chapter 16: “Stabilizing” Commodities
  q(5, 16, 'easy', 'What role do speculators play for farmers, according to Hazlitt?', 'They bear price risks and steady prices', ['They cheat farmers at harvest time', 'They create shortages for profit', 'None: they only trade paper'],
    'The better they foresee prices, the more they profit and the smaller the swings. They buy when crops are dumped, so prices hold steady all year.', 'Someone has to carry the risk.'),
  q(5, 16, 'hard', 'Careful studies found the price rise after harvest didn’t quite cover storage costs. What does that mean?', 'Speculators actually subsidized farmers', ['Farmers were cheated by speculators', 'Storage was too expensive to use', 'The government set the prices'],
    'Out of persistent over-optimism, not intention. Hazlitt adds that more has been spent prospecting for gold or oil than the gold or oil was worth.', 'Who lost money on the storage?'),
  q(5, 16, 'medium', 'What does the government’s “ever-normal granary” become, in Hazlitt’s words?', 'An ever-political granary', ['A truly normal granary', 'A private grain market', 'A charity for the poor'],
    'Politicians set the “fair” price above what supply and demand justify, so stocks pile up: it turns into an ever-abnormal granary.', 'Who sets the price, and why?'),
  q(5, 16, 'medium', 'Holding part of a crop off the market this year raises the price now. What happens next year?', 'An artificial surplus and lower prices', ['Prices keep rising forever', 'Farmers grow less of it on their own accord', 'Nothing: the crop is eaten'],
    'The artificial shortage of this year means an artificial surplus the next. With American cotton, it destroyed foreign markets and spurred cotton growing abroad.', 'Where does the stored crop go eventually?'),
  q(5, 16, 'hard', 'Why is a uniform output restriction worse than a fall in price on a free market?', 'It keeps inefficient producers in business', ['It lowers prices for consumers', 'It helps the best farmers most', 'It is far easier for the government to enforce'],
    'A free market drives out the high-cost farmers on the poorest land and lets the best expand. A uniform cut holds back the efficient and props up the rest.', 'Who leaves the market, in each case?'),
  q(5, 16, 'hard', 'What happened to the British rubber and American cotton restriction schemes, according to Hazlitt?', 'Prices finally collapsed catastrophically', ['They stabilized world prices for several decades', 'They made both nations rich', 'They were never tried at all'],
    'Surpluses piled up, or producers outside the schemes expanded enormously. “Stabilization” brought far greater instability than the free market would have.', 'What do high prices do to other growers?'),
  q(5, 16, 'medium', 'What did postwar planners mean by “free” trade under international commodity controls, Hazlitt suspects?', 'Freedom for bureaucrats to decide for people', ['Freedom to trade at any price one likes', 'Freedom for farmers to grow what they wish', 'Freedom to move capital anywhere'],
    'Not the freedom of ordinary people to buy, sell, lend and settle as they please. He feared living standards would fall along with liberties.', 'Whose freedom?'),

  // Chapter 17: Government Price-Fixing
  q(5, 17, 'easy', 'What happens when the government holds a price below the market level?', 'Demand rises and supply falls', ['Supply rises and demand falls', 'Both rise in equal amounts', 'Nothing changes for long'],
    'The cheaper good is bought faster, and producing it is discouraged: profits shrink, marginal producers quit. The result is a shortage.', 'What does a bargain do to buyers, and to sellers?'),
  q(5, 17, 'medium', 'Beef is capped at 50 cents to stop purchasing power, not need, deciding who eats it. What’s the flaw?', 'It applies at any price above zero', ['Beef is never really a necessity', 'The rich don’t actually eat much beef', 'Need can’t be measured at all'],
    'The argument holds as long as anything at all is charged. It would only cease to apply if beef were given away.', 'Does the argument stop at 50 cents?'),
  q(5, 17, 'medium', 'How does price-fixing necessities, but not luxuries, backfire?', 'It shifts production toward luxuries', ['It makes necessities more plentiful', 'It lowers the price of luxuries', 'It raises wages in necessities'],
    'Limiting the pay and profits of those making necessities discourages them, while making luxuries becomes relatively more attractive.', 'Where would producers rather work?'),
  q(5, 17, 'medium', 'Why does rationing only do part of the job a free price would?', 'It limits demand but doesn’t raise supply', ['It raises supply but not demand', 'It requires far too many coupons and clerks', 'It applies only in wartime'],
    'Rationing creates a double currency of money plus coupons. A higher price would have both limited demand and stimulated supply.', 'What does a higher price do for producers?'),
  q(5, 17, 'hard', 'A subsidy is paid to milk producers to keep milk cheap. Who is really being subsidized?', 'The consumers', ['The milk producers', 'The government', 'The dairy workers'],
    'Producers get no more than the free market price; consumers pay less. And those with most purchasing power buy most of it, unless it is also rationed.', 'Who pays less than the market price?'),
  q(5, 17, 'hard', '“A power over a man’s subsistence amounts to a power over his will.” Who wrote it?', 'Alexander Hamilton', ['Thomas Jefferson', 'Adam Smith', 'Frédéric Bastiat'],
    'Hazlitt quotes the Federalist Papers: thorough price control ends in a regimented economy, with every firm and worker at the government’s mercy.', 'The Federalist Papers.'),
  q(5, 17, 'medium', 'What harm did black markets do, even though they kept people fed in postwar Europe?', 'They rewarded dishonesty and bad firms', ['They made all goods far too cheap to produce', 'They caused the price ceilings', 'None: they were purely helpful'],
    'Established firms cut production while fly-by-night concerns made inferior goods at higher cost. Lawbreaking became the condition of business.', 'Who thrives when breaking the law is required?'),
  q(5, 17, 'medium', 'What really causes prices to rise, according to Hazlitt?', 'Scarce goods or surplus money', ['Greedy businessmen and retailers', 'Speculators', 'High wages'],
    'Legal ceilings can cure neither. They only make the shortage of goods worse.', 'Too few goods, or too much of what?'),
  q(5, 17, 'hard', 'Hazlitt says each of us is a Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde. In what way?', 'Producer, consumer and taxpayer differ', ['We are honest at home, greedy at work', 'We save in good times and spend in bad', 'We vote one way and act another'],
    'As a producer he wants inflation for his own product; as a consumer, price ceilings; as a consumer he backs subsidies, as a taxpayer he resents them.', 'Which role are you thinking in at the moment?'),

  // Part VI: Wages, Unions and Profits (chapters 18–21). Chapter 18: Minimum Wage Laws
  q(6, 18, 'easy', 'What is a wage, at bottom, according to Hazlitt?', 'A price', ['A gift', 'A right', 'A tax'],
    'Calling the price of labor’s services by a different name has kept people from seeing the same principles govern both.', 'The price of what?'),
  q(6, 18, 'easy', 'A law says no one may be paid less than $30 a week. What happens to a worker worth less than that to employers?', 'He isn’t hired at all', ['He is paid $30 anyway', 'He gets a raise to $30', 'His employer pays a fine'],
    '“You cannot make a man worth a given amount by making it illegal for anyone to offer him anything less.” For a low wage you substitute unemployment.', 'Will anyone pay more than he’s worth to them?'),
  q(6, 18, 'medium', 'Some say an industry that can only pay starvation wages may as well die. What does that overlook?', 'Those were its workers’ best alternatives', ['That such wages are always fair', 'That the industry will survive anyway', 'That workers prefer low wages'],
    'Otherwise they’d have gone elsewhere. Pushed into worse options, their competition drives down pay there too, and consumers lose the product.', 'Why did the workers take those jobs?'),
  q(6, 18, 'medium', 'A minimum wage forbids work at $25 a week; relief pays $18. What has society done?', 'Paid him less to idle than to work', ['Saved the man from poverty wages', 'Created a new public job just for him', 'Raised his income to $30'],
    'It loses his services, and he loses the independence and self-respect of supporting himself, for less money.', 'Compare $25 earned with $18 for nothing.'),
  q(6, 18, 'hard', 'If relief pays $30 a week and a job pays $40, what is the worker really being asked to work for, as he sees it?', '$10 a week', ['$40 a week', '$30 a week', '$70 a week'],
    'Whatever the relief, everyone works only for the difference between his wage and the relief payment.', 'He can get the rest for doing nothing.'),
  q(6, 18, 'medium', 'What distinguishes many reformers from their critics, according to Hazlitt?', 'Greater impatience, not greater kindness', ['Greater knowledge of economics', 'Greater and more sincere concern for the poor', 'Greater experience in business'],
    'Among men of goodwill, wanting everyone well off is taken for granted. The question is the means.', 'Not who cares more.'),
  q(6, 18, 'hard', 'What, for Hazlitt, is the best way to raise wages?', 'Raise the productivity of labor', ['Pass higher minimum wages', 'Strengthen the labor unions', 'Cut the length of the workweek'],
    'More capital, inventions, better management, more industry and training. “Real wages come out of production, not out of government decrees.”', 'We can’t pay labor more than it produces.'),

  // Chapter 19: Do Unions Really Raise Wages?
  q(6, 19, 'easy', 'Why were American wages far higher than English and German wages, though their labor movements were more advanced?', 'American labor was more productive', ['American unions were stronger', 'American laws set higher minimums', 'American employers were kinder'],
    'Wages are basically determined by labor productivity, not by the strength of the labor movement.', 'What determines wages, at bottom?'),
  q(6, 19, 'medium', 'Why do employers tend to bid workers up to their full worth, according to Hazlitt?', 'Because each wants the most profit', ['Because employers are kind', 'Because the law requires them to do it', 'Because unions force them'],
    'Why make $1 a week from a worker and let a rival make $2 from him? Self-interest, not generosity, pushes wages up.', 'Would you let a rival hire him away?'),
  q(6, 19, 'medium', 'What legitimate function does Hazlitt grant to unions?', 'Getting members their true market value', ['Raising all wages above market levels', 'Running the industry’s management', 'Deciding who may enter a trade'],
    'A lone worker may not know his worth and is in a weaker position. A known standard wage can even out bargaining power and risks.', 'A lone worker is at a disadvantage.'),
  q(6, 19, 'hard', 'Against whom are violent strike pickets mainly used, according to Hazlitt?', 'Other workers who want the jobs', ['The employer and his managers', 'The government and its police', 'Consumers of the product'],
    'Those willing to take the vacated jobs at the old wage are shoved into worse jobs so the strikers can keep a privileged position by force.', 'Who wants the jobs the strikers left?'),
  q(6, 19, 'hard', 'Six unions win raises from 0 to 50 percent, averaging 25, and prices rise accordingly. Who comes out ahead?', 'Only the strongest unions, and barely', ['Every union, by about 25 percent', 'All workers equally, in the same proportion', 'The farm hands most of all'],
    'Farm hands with no raise are worse off; store workers with 10 percent too. Even the 50 percent gain is far smaller in reality, before counting unemployment.', 'The cost of living rose 25 percent.'),
  q(6, 19, 'hard', 'Railway employment fell from 1,685,000 in 1920 to 699,000 in 1938. What happened to the average hourly wage?', 'It rose from 66 to 74 cents', ['It fell by half', 'It stayed at exactly 66 cents an hour', 'It doubled to $1.32'],
    'Higher wage rates came with drastic losses of railway jobs.', 'Fewer workers, at higher rates.'),
  q(6, 19, 'medium', 'Why can labor’s “exploitation” of capital already invested last only a short time?', 'Investors stop putting in new capital', ['The law soon forbids it', 'Workers grow tired of striking for higher pay', 'Capital can’t be lowered at all'],
    'Trapped capital keeps running if it earns anything at all, but no new money goes in, and worn-out equipment isn’t replaced.', 'Would you invest more where returns are cut?'),
  q(6, 19, 'hard', 'What fallacy, Hazlitt says, credits unions with the great rise in wages over the previous half century?', 'Post hoc ergo propter hoc', ['The fallacy of composition', 'The broken-window fallacy', 'The gambler’s fallacy'],
    'Unions grew while wages rose; the real cause was capital investment and technological advance.', 'After this, therefore because of this.'),
  q(6, 19, 'medium', 'Which union practice does Hazlitt not count against productivity?', 'Early standards protecting health', ['Promotion by seniority, not merit', 'Opposing new machinery', 'Equal pay regardless of output'],
    'In their early history unions did much for members’ health and even helped production. Many later practices, he argues, held real wages down.', 'Something they did well in their early days.'),
  q(6, 19, 'easy', '“Work creates work.” What false idea does that answer?', 'That there’s a fixed fund of jobs', ['That machines create jobs', 'That wages must always rise every year', 'That unions raise wages'],
    'What A produces is the demand for what B produces. There is no limit to the work to be done.', 'The “job fund.”'),

  // Chapter 20: “Enough to Buy Back the Product”
  q(6, 20, 'easy', 'Which concept did the classical economists put in place of “just” prices and wages?', 'Functional prices and wages', ['Fixed prices and wages', 'Fair-trade prices set by the state', 'Subsistence wages'],
    'Functional prices bring the largest production and sales; functional wages, the highest employment and largest payrolls.', 'They work, rather than seem fair.'),
  q(6, 20, 'medium', 'Should Ford workers earn enough to buy Fords, and Cadillac workers Cadillacs?', 'No: the idea makes no sense', ['Yes: that’s the whole point', 'Only in the car industry', 'Only for skilled workers'],
    'Makers of mink coats needn’t buy back mink coats. The national product is bought by everyone who helps make it.', 'Who buys mink coats?'),
  q(6, 20, 'medium', 'In an exchange economy, Hazlitt notes, everybody’s income is also what?', 'Somebody else’s cost', ['A tax on someone else', 'Money taken from profits', 'The same as his savings'],
    'Every wage increase not matched by higher productivity raises costs, leading to higher prices, shrinking markets or unemployment.', 'Who pays your wage?'),
  q(6, 20, 'hard', 'Douglas and Pigou put the elasticity of demand for labor at −3 to −4. What does that mean for wages pushed above productivity?', 'Jobs fall three to four times as much', ['Jobs rise three to four times as much', 'Wages fall by three or four percent', 'Nothing changes for workers'],
    'Douglas reached it from statistics, Pigou almost purely by deduction. Total payrolls would fall.', 'Small rise in rates, big drop in what?'),
  q(6, 20, 'hard', 'Direct labor is a third of car-making costs. Why won’t a 30 percent wage rise add only 10 percent to car prices?', 'Labor is in materials, parts and transport too', ['Car makers will double their profits to pay for it', 'Taxes rise along with the wages', 'Steel prices are fixed by law'],
    'Wages were about 69 percent of national income from 1929 to 1943. Counting indirect labor costs, prices rise nearly 20 percent, and in the long run about 30.', 'Who made the steel and the tires?'),
  q(6, 20, 'medium', 'What are the best profits, from labor’s standpoint as well as industry’s, according to Hazlitt?', 'Those that bring the most employers', ['The lowest possible profits', 'Profits capped by law at 10 percent', 'No profits at all'],
    'The best prices aren’t the highest; the best wages aren’t the highest; the best profits aren’t the lowest. “We must run the economy for everybody.”', 'What makes people become employers?'),

  // Chapter 21: The Function of Profits
  q(6, 21, 'easy', 'How large were the net profits of incorporated business, 1929–1943, as a share of national income?', 'Under 5 percent', ['About 25 percent', 'About 50 percent', 'Over 69 percent'],
    'Yet profits are the income people resent most. There’s a word “profiteer,” but none for “wageer,” or “losseer.”', 'Smaller than most people think.'),
  q(6, 21, 'medium', 'Of ten grocery stores opening, how many could expect to celebrate their fourth birthday, per the TNEC studies?', 'Only four', ['Nine', 'All ten of them', 'Seven'],
    'About seven of ten survive into a second year; only four reach a fourth. And from 1930 to 1938, loss-making corporations outnumbered profitable ones every year.', 'Fewer than half.'),
  q(6, 21, 'hard', 'Some economists believe that, after all losses, interest and imputed wages, entrepreneurs as a whole earn what?', 'No net profit, perhaps a loss', ['Huge, steady profits every year', 'Exactly 10 percent a year', 'Whatever the state allows'],
    'Not out of philanthropy, but because optimism leads them into ventures that fail. Hazlitt cites Frank Knight’s Risk, Uncertainty and Profit.', 'Many ventures fail.'),
  q(6, 21, 'medium', 'If there’s no profit in making an article, what does that signal?', 'Resources are worth more elsewhere', ['The makers are being exploited', 'The price must be raised by law', 'Demand is about to boom'],
    'The value of the resources used up exceeds the value of the article. Profits steer labor and capital to the output consumers want.', 'Compare the cost with the product’s value.'),
  q(6, 21, 'hard', 'Besides guiding what is made, what other function do profits serve, for Hazlitt?', 'Pressure to keep finding economies', ['Funding government programs', 'Rewarding firms for their size', 'Keeping prices permanently high'],
    'In good times to earn more, in normal times to stay ahead, in bad times to survive. Profits show not just what to make but the cheapest way to make it.', 'Losses are a powerful motivator.'),

  // Part VII: Inflation, Saving, and the Lesson Restated (chapters 22–24). Chapter 22: The Mirage of Inflation
  q(7, 22, 'easy', 'What is the oldest and most stubborn error behind the appeal of inflation, for Hazlitt?', 'Confusing money with wealth', ['Trusting the gold standard', 'Saving too much money', 'Paying wages too low'],
    'Real wealth is what is produced and consumed: food, clothes, houses, railways, books. Printing paper doesn’t multiply them.', 'Is a dollar the same thing as a loaf of bread?'),
  q(7, 22, 'medium', 'New money is printed to pay war contractors. Who gains, and who loses?', 'First receivers gain; the last lose', ['Everyone gains equally', 'Only the government gains, at everyone’s cost', 'Nobody gains or loses'],
    'Group A’s income rises before prices do; group D’s hasn’t risen when prices are up 20 percent. Even when all catch up, D never recovers its losses in between.', 'Who spends the new money first?'),
  q(7, 22, 'hard', 'Why can’t the value of money be controlled precisely during an inflation?', 'It depends on what people expect and value', ['Mints can’t print fast enough', 'Banks hide how much money they really hold', 'Gold prices change every day'],
    'It isn’t mechanical: it depends on the money’s quality and on expectations, so in hyperinflation its value falls far faster than its quantity grows.', 'Is it just a matter of quantity?'),
  q(7, 22, 'medium', 'What does inflation really do to “get the wheels turning,” according to Hazlitt?', 'Lower real wages by raising prices', ['Create new purchasing power', 'Increase the supply of goods', 'Raise profits and wages together'],
    'It restores a workable relation between prices and costs by deception. Cutting wage rates would do it more directly and honestly, and labor isn’t fooled for long.', 'What happens to wages in terms of goods?'),
  q(7, 22, 'medium', 'Hazlitt compares inflation’s corrections to what wartime trick?', 'Turning the clocks ahead an hour', ['Printing ration coupons', 'Painting fake tanks to fool the enemy', 'Rationing gasoline'],
    'It’s like getting people up an hour earlier by making them believe it’s eight o’clock when it’s really seven.', 'Daylight saving time.'),
  q(7, 22, 'hard', '“Inflation is the opium of the people.” What does Hazlitt mean?', 'It dulls the pain of falling real income', ['It makes people too lazy to work', 'It is pushed on the people by greedy bankers', 'It makes everyone really richer'],
    'A clerk raised from $25 to $35 feels better off though living costs doubled. Its confusion is exactly its political function for planned-economy governments.', 'An anesthetic for an operation.'),
  q(7, 22, 'medium', 'What kind of tax is inflation, in Hazlitt’s analysis?', 'Perhaps the worst, hardest on the poor', ['A fair tax that falls on the rich alone', 'No tax at all, just growth', 'A tax only on foreigners'],
    'Like a flat sales tax on bread and diamonds alike, or a capital levy without exemptions, with a rate no one knows in advance.', 'Who can least afford higher prices?'),
  q(7, 22, 'hard', 'What does Hazlitt say inflation ends in, after discouraging thrift and rewarding speculation?', 'Disillusion and collapse', ['A stable new prosperity', 'Lower prices for everyone', 'A balanced budget'],
    'It plants the seeds of fascism and communism and leads men to demand totalitarian controls.', 'Dead Sea fruit turns to dust and ashes.'),

  // Chapter 23: The Assault on Saving
  q(7, 23, 'easy', 'In Bastiat’s story of two brothers, Alvin spends everything and Benjamin saves. Who provides more jobs?', 'Benjamin, over time', ['Alvin, by far', 'Both exactly equally, forever', 'Neither one of them'],
    'Benjamin’s savings are invested in capital goods that employ just as many. A dozen years on, Alvin is broke, and Benjamin’s growing income provides more jobs than ever.', 'Who is still spending in twelve years?'),
  q(7, 23, 'medium', 'What is “saving,” in the modern world, according to Hazlitt?', 'Another form of spending', ['Money taken out of circulation', 'A cause of depressions', 'Hoarding cash under the mattress'],
    'Saved money is turned over to someone else to spend on means of production: buildings, machines, ships. It employs as many as spending on consumption.', 'Where does the money in a bank go?'),
  q(7, 23, 'hard', 'People stop buying after a downturn begins. Is that the “saving” that causes depressions?', 'No: it’s a consequence of depression', ['Yes: it starts every depression', 'Yes: but only in the big industrial cities', 'No: it never happens at all'],
    'They hold money expecting prices to fall, or fear losing their jobs. It may prolong a slump, but doesn’t start it. Blaming it is like blaming low apple prices on buyers, not the bumper crop.', 'Which came first?'),
  q(7, 23, 'medium', 'A nation saves 20 percent of output each year. In Hazlitt’s table, what happens by the eleventh year?', 'Consumption equals year one’s total output', ['Consumption has shrunk a little every year', 'All the savings sit unused', 'The capital goods are worn out'],
    'Production rises from 100 to 125, and consumers’ goods from 80 to 100. The cake grows every year, and more of it is eaten.', 'A larger cake each year.'),
  q(7, 23, 'medium', 'What brings savings and investment into balance, according to Hazlitt?', 'The interest rate', ['The central bank', 'Government projects', 'The tax rate'],
    'Savings and investment are the supply of and demand for new capital, and the interest rate is simply the price of loaned capital.', 'The price of loaned capital.'),
  q(7, 23, 'hard', 'What happens when interest rates are held artificially low, for Hazlitt?', 'Demand for capital rises, supply falls', ['Saving rises and debt falls', 'Prices fall steadily for all', 'Investment becomes much safer for everyone'],
    'Like any price held below market, it causes a shortage. It can be kept up only by injecting new money instead of savings, like adding water to create the illusion of more milk.', 'What happens to any price held too low?'),
  q(7, 23, 'medium', 'Is there a limit to how much new capital can be absorbed, according to Hazlitt?', 'No: not while costs can still be cut', ['Yes: it was reached by 1945', 'Yes: as soon as every family has a home', 'Only in rich countries'],
    'There won’t be a surplus until the most backward country is as well equipped as the most advanced and human ingenuity hits a dead end.', 'Can costs always be lowered further?'),
  q(7, 23, 'hard', 'How could American car makers pay the world’s highest wages yet undersell the rest of the world?', 'More capital per worker and per car', ['Lower taxes than other countries', 'Government export subsidies', 'Cheaper steel from abroad'],
    'Capital lowered unit costs, cheapening cars for consumers and raising the productivity, and so the wages, of the workers.', 'What did each worker have to work with?'),

  // Chapter 24: The Lesson Restated
  q(7, 24, 'easy', 'What is economics, as Hazlitt restates it at the end?', 'A science of recognizing secondary consequences', ['A science of managing the money supply', 'The study of how big businesses make their profits', 'A branch of politics and law'],
    'It traces the effects of a policy not only on a special interest in the short run, but on the general interest in the long run.', 'The one lesson, again.'),
  q(7, 24, 'medium', 'Hazlitt says increasing “credit” is another name for increasing debt. What’s another name for increasing subsidies?', 'Increasing taxes', ['Increasing wealth', 'Increasing exports', 'Increasing saving'],
    'Every proposal has a reverse side: higher farm prices are dearer food for city workers; higher wages, higher costs of production.', 'Who pays the subsidies?'),
  q(7, 24, 'hard', 'Who is William Graham Sumner’s “Forgotten Man”?', 'C, who pays for what A and B do for X', ['X, the poor man A and B want to help', 'A, the reformer who proposes the law', 'The unemployed worker of the 1930s'],
    'Ironically, when the phrase was revived in the 1930s it was applied to X, and C, made to support still more X’s, was more forgotten than ever.', 'Who pays for the reformers’ generosity?'),
  q(7, 24, 'medium', 'What does depth in economics bring men back to, according to Hazlitt?', 'Common sense', ['Religion', 'Socialism', 'Mathematics'],
    'A little economics leads to absurd conclusions, like broken windows being good. Depth means looking for all the consequences, as Adam Smith did.', 'He echoes Bacon on philosophy.'),
  q(7, 24, 'hard', 'Why does the central fallacy arise systematically, not by accident, for Hazlitt?', 'Each wants scarcity in what he sells', ['People are taught bad economics at school', 'Governments hide the true figures', 'Most people dislike thinking about money'],
    'I make one thing and buy everything else. I want abundance in all I buy, and scarcity in the one thing I sell.', 'You sell one thing and buy everything else.'),
  q(7, 24, 'medium', 'What does Hazlitt call the doctrine behind tariffs, machine-breaking and burning coffee?', 'The insane doctrine of wealth through scarcity', ['The sensible doctrine of fair prices', 'The classical doctrine of free trade', 'The modern and enlightened doctrine of full employment'],
    'It may be privately true for one group that makes its product scarce while all else stays abundant. It is always publicly false: applied all round, it is economic suicide.', 'Getting rich by making things scarce.'),
  q(7, 24, 'hard', 'A sudden rise in public morals would hurt whom, among others, according to Hazlitt?', 'Preachers and reformers', ['Only bartenders and gamblers', 'Nobody at all', 'Only the police'],
    'Almost any fulfillment of a human need hurts those invested in meeting it, even those whose business is improving morals.', 'Who would have less to complain about?'),
  q(7, 24, 'medium', 'What does Hazlitt call the goal of economic science, in his final line?', 'To see the problem whole, not in fragments', ['To make the nation rich quickly', 'To abolish government entirely, step by step', 'To predict prices exactly'],
    '“To see the problem as a whole, and not in fragments: that is the goal of economic science.”', 'The whole, not the parts.'),
];

